# Change the canon

The canon is in `canon/`, listed in `canon/manifest.yaml`. Each member pins
one commit of this repository in `fleet/pin.yaml`. A release moves every
member's pin to one new commit.

## 1. Change

On a branch here, either:

- **edit a file under `canon/`** (or add one, with its manifest entry), or
- **take mechmaker's**: `just update-template`. It brings mechmaker's new
  `mech_shared.yaml` and `history.yaml` and their sha256s. Read the
  mechmaker release notes for what changed.

Then `just canon-refresh` (rewrites the sha256 of what you changed) and
`just qc`. A change to `mech_shared.yaml` or `history.yaml` is a change to
every member's data model: say in the pull request what records must do.

## 2. Release

Merge the pull request. Its merge commit on `main` is the release.

## 3. Sync each member

For each active member, in its checkout on a fresh branch:

```bash
just sync <member> <checkout>           # the plan
just sync <member> <checkout> --apply
```

Then, in the member: `just qc`, migrate any records the change requires,
commit the canon files and `fleet/pin.yaml`, and open a pull request. In a
member, sync-mech from mechmaker must not take canon files: they come from
here.

## 4. Audit

As members merge, `just fetch && just audit`. It reports members on
different pins until the last one merges; that is the release in progress.
It passes when all are on the new commit.

## Rolling back

Sync every member to the earlier commit: `just sync <member> <checkout>
--ref <full commit> --apply`. Then revert the change here.
